  <h2>The brief</h2>
  <p>
    An external client asked our team whether low-carbon, fuel-cell grade hydrogen could be made from agricultural residue in Southern Ontario, at 1,000 kg/day and at least 99.99% purity. We selected corn stover as the feedstock and a gasification route: steam and oxygen fluidized-bed gasification, gas cleanup, methane reforming, two-stage water-gas shift, pressure swing adsorption (PSA), then high-pressure storage. Our recommendation was to advance to front-end engineering design (FEED).
  </p>

  <h2>What I did</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Sized the 4-bed PSA system per ASME BPVC: adsorbent mass, vessel geometry and wall thickness, for 99.99% H₂ purity at 95% recovery.</li>
    <li>Built mass balances in Excel and Aspen, and contributed to the PFD, the P&amp;ID and the HAZOP.</li>
    <li>Created equipment drawings and 3D models in AutoCAD Plant 3D and prepared equipment data sheets.</li>
    <li>Wrote the design report and reviewed the design with the client at milestone meetings as task lead for part of the project.</li>
    <li>Co-developed the CAPEX and OPEX estimates and the 20-year discounted cash flow model, with sensitivity analysis on price and discount rate.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The PSA unit</h2>
  <p>
    Shifted syngas reaches the PSA at about 10 bar. Activated carbon holds back CO₂ and heavier components while hydrogen passes through as the light product. Each bed cycles through adsorption, depressurization to about 2 bar, purge with a small share of product hydrogen, and repressurization, on short cycles so the downstream buffer sees near-steady flow. Multiple beds in parallel keep the product continuous.
  </p>

  <figure>
    <img src="/figures/capstone/plant-3d-layout.png" alt="AutoCAD Plant 3D model of the plant layout showing the gasifier, filters, reactors, knockout drum, PSA columns, compressors and storage tanks." width="833" height="332" loading="lazy" />
    <figcaption><b>Fig. 02</b>3D plant layout from AutoCAD Plant 3D, used to check safe distances and piping runs.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>The economics</h2>
  <p>
    Capital came to $18.0M as an AACE Class 4 estimate with $6.0M a year to operate. Over 20 years the discounted cash flow model gives a $19.3M NPV, a 19.3% IRR and a 7.4-year discounted payback. The two 19.3 figures are a coincidence of the model, not a typo.
  </p>
  <p class="callout">
    The case rests on the client's hydrogen selling price assumption. Our sensitivity analysis found the hurdle rate and the hydrogen price moved the result most, with low-price scenarios making the project infeasible, while feedstock and maintenance costs barely moved it.
  </p>
</Project>
